# Agent settings and permissions

Read this before changing `.claude/`, permissions and agent hooks, and when a developer wants to change whether the agent may commit or push.

## What lives where

| Path | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `AGENTS.md` | Rules, read by Claude Code and Codex |
| `.claude/settings.json` | Claude Code hooks and permissions for sessions from the root, shared by the team |
| `.claude/settings.local.json` | The developer's personal settings, in `.gitignore` |
| `.claude/hooks/agent-git-guard.mjs` | Hook that decides whether the agent may commit and push, refuses Claude attribution and refuses merges into `main`, `dev` and rest branches |
| `.claude/skills/` | Skills: `bootstrap`, `add-ast-grep-rule` and, after `mise run kit:install`, `openspec-*` from OpenSpec |
| `.claude/commands/`, `.claude/agents/` | After `mise run kit:install`: the `/opsx:*` and `/sdd:*` commands and the sdd-kit reviewer agents |
| `openspec/tooling/hooks/` | sdd-kit hooks, wired into `.claude/settings.json` by the kit's installer |

OpenSpec and sdd-kit files are overwritten on update; do not edit them by hand.

## AGENTS.md without CLAUDE.md

Claude Code reads `AGENTS.md` natively since version 2.1.277. If the root has `CLAUDE.md`, `.claude/CLAUDE.md` or `CLAUDE.local.md`, reading `AGENTS.md` is turned off, so the root has no such files and `mise run lint` catches them. A clone's `CLAUDE.md` lives below the root and does not turn reading off. Rationale in [ADR 0002](../adr/0002-only-agents-md.md).

## Agent commit and push

{% if agent_git_default == 'on' -%}
In this project the agent commits and pushes by default, in the clones and in the meta-repository: the shared `.claude/settings.json` sets both variables to `1`. A developer who does not want this turns either off for themselves in their own `.claude/settings.local.json`, whose `env` overrides the shared one. Commit and push are set separately.

```json
{
  "env": {
    "METAREPO_AGENT_COMMIT": "0",
    "METAREPO_AGENT_PUSH": "0"
  }
}
```
{%- else -%}
By default the agent does not commit or push, neither in the clones nor in the meta-repository. Each developer enables this for themselves in their own `.claude/settings.local.json`. Commit and push are enabled separately.

```json
{
  "env": {
    "METAREPO_AGENT_COMMIT": "1",
    "METAREPO_AGENT_PUSH": "1"
  }
}
```
{%- endif %}

- `METAREPO_AGENT_COMMIT=1` allows `git commit`. It does not allow push.
- `METAREPO_AGENT_PUSH=1` allows `git push`.
- Any other value, or no value, refuses the command.
- A commit while the repository is on `main`, and a push to `main`, are always refused, whatever the settings.
- A variable written in the command itself (`METAREPO_AGENT_COMMIT=1 git commit`) does not count. The hook looks only at the session environment.

The hook `.claude/hooks/agent-git-guard.mjs` decides. It runs on every Bash command, finds the repository from `git -C <folder>`, from a `cd <folder>` before the command, or from the session folder, and refuses the command before it runs with a message naming the required setting or `main`. The refusal on `main` names the branch to start from, read from the clone's branch model in `repos.toml`. The hook reads the command text, so it will not see a commit made from a script (see "What the hook does not see" below). The clones' own hooks and the branch rules remain the safeguard. Reasons for the decision in [ADR 0004](../adr/0004-agent-commit-push-opt-in.md).

After changing `.claude/settings.local.json`, restart the Claude Code session so the environment is refreshed.

## No Claude attribution

Commits and pull requests carry no Claude attribution in any repository. Two things enforce this.

The shared `.claude/settings.json` turns off the lines Claude Code adds by default:

```json
{
  "attribution": { "commit": "", "pr": "" },
  "includeCoAuthoredBy": false
}
```

`includeCoAuthoredBy` is the older setting, kept for developers on older Claude Code versions.

The hook `.claude/hooks/agent-git-guard.mjs` also refuses attribution before the command runs. The check:

- Covers `git commit` and `gh pr create`, `gh pr edit`, `gh pr comment` and `gh pr review`, in the meta-repository and in the clones.
- Reads the text of `-m`/`--message`, `--trailer` and `--author` for git, and `--title`/`-t` and `--body`/`-b` for gh. It also reads the file named by `-F`/`--file` (git) or `--body-file`/`-F` (gh), relative to the command's folder (`git -C`, `cd` or the session folder). With `-` it checks the heredoc that feeds the command.
- Refuses text with a `Generated with Claude Code` line, a `Co-Authored-By:` line naming Claude or `anthropic.com`, or the words Claude or Anthropic anywhere else. Letter case does not matter.
- Allows paths ending in `CLAUDE.md` or `CLAUDE.local.md`, paths containing `.claude/`, and the name `Claude Code`. `Claude Code` is still refused inside the `Generated with Claude Code` line.
- Refuses text it cannot see: a `git commit` with no `-m` or `-F` (it would open an editor), `gh pr create --fill`, `--fill-first` and `--fill-verbose`, and `--editor` or `--web` on `gh pr create` and `gh pr comment`. A commit that reuses a message with `--no-edit` or `-C` passes.

The refusal names what it found, for example `Co-Authored-By` or the word as written. When a legitimate text is refused, for example a commit about an Anthropic API key, rephrase it or ask the developer to commit by hand. The permission refusals come first, so a developer without commit permission sees the usual message. Then comes the attribution check, then the `main` rules.

## Merging stays with the developer

The agent never merges a pull request and never merges into `main`, `dev` or a clone's rest branch. The hook refuses, whatever the personal settings:

| Command | Refused when | Message |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `gh pr merge` | Always, in every repository | The developer merges pull requests by hand |
| `git merge` | The target repository is on `main`, `dev` or its rest branch from `repos.toml` | Names the branch and says the developer merges by hand |

A `git merge` on any other branch passes, such as merging a work branch into its merge branch `merge/<name>-into-dev` ([branches.md](branches.md)). `git merge --abort` and `--quit` also pass.

## What the hook does not see

The hook reads the command text. It does not see a commit, merge or pull request made from inside a script, a text built by a command substitution such as `--body "$(cat body.md)"`, or a comment or merge made through `gh api`. The clones' own hooks, the branch rules and the developer's review remain the safeguard.

## sdd-kit hooks

The sdd-kit installer merges its hooks into `.claude/settings.json`: the answer gate, the archive gate, protection against actions outside the project, and others, plus read-only permissions for its checks. The `agent-git-guard` hook sits next to them as a separate Bash entry. After `mise run kit:update`, check that the hook entry is still there; `mise run test` checks it.

## Permissions

The template's own permissions in `.claude/settings.json` allow only exact read-only commands: `mise run status`, `lint`, `test`, `check`, `repos:check` and the registry's `check` and `list`. Add a permission only for a command that changes nothing, and write it as exactly as possible.

## Skills

A skill is a folder with a `SKILL.md` whose front matter has `name` and `description`. The description decides when the agent loads the skill, so it names the situation. The meta-repository has two skills of its own: `bootstrap`, the AI onboarding that reads the clones and configures their mise tasks, the docs and the OpenSpec context, asking questions with options; and `add-ast-grep-rule`, which goes from the idea of a shared rule to its test.
